{#
  Sets the colour scheme before the first paint, so there is no flash of the wrong theme.

  It must stay inline and stay in <head>: an external file is a round trip, and by the time it
  lands the wrong colours are already on screen. The CSP transform hashes whatever this renders,
  so editing it cannot break the policy.

  `<html>` already carries `dark`, so this only ever needs to take it off.

  The `?theme=` branch is the handoff between tzortzoglou.eu and app.tzortzoglou.eu. localStorage
  is per-origin, so a choice made on one is invisible to the other, and crossing over used to
  flip you back to dark in your face. The header appends the parameter to its cross-property link
  (see scripts/chrome.js), this adopts it, and this then takes it back out of the address bar.

  The cleanup happens here rather than on DOMContentLoaded for the same reason the theme itself
  is decided here: this block already has to run before the first paint, and leaving `?theme=dark`
  in the address bar until the document is ready means it is in whatever the visitor copies,
  shares or bookmarks in between. It also used to be a race against the counter, which read
  location.search as it loaded - that counter is gone, and the reason above is the one that
  survives it. Nothing now reads the query string on either property: the page-view beacon sends
  location.pathname and never location.href, deliberately.

  Whichever choice was made most recently wins, wherever it was made - hence `theme-at`, written
  beside `theme` by the toggle and carried alongside it. The first version instead let the
  receiving origin's own preference win, which sounded careful and was wrong: the toggle writes
  that preference, so using it once on the app disabled the handoff on that browser forever. A
  feature that works exactly once is worse than no feature, because you stop expecting it.

  An origin holding a `theme` with no `theme-at` predates this and counts as timestamp 0, so the
  first deliberate choice made anywhere wins and the pair converges. Both are the same browser,
  so there is no clock to disagree about.

  A cookie on .tzortzoglou.eu would have been less code and would have needed none of this. It
  was rejected because /cookies/ says this site sets none, and that claim is worth more.
#}
<script>(function(){var q=null;try{q=new URLSearchParams(location.search);}catch(e){}var inc=q&&q.get('theme');if(inc!=='light'&&inc!=='dark'){inc=null;}var incAt=q&&+q.get('theme-at')||0;var t=null,at=0;try{t=localStorage.getItem('theme');at=+localStorage.getItem('theme-at')||0;}catch(e){}if(inc&&(!t||incAt>at)){t=inc;try{localStorage.setItem('theme',t);localStorage.setItem('theme-at',String(incAt));}catch(e){}}if(t==='light'){document.documentElement.classList.remove('dark');}if(q&&q.has('theme')){try{q.delete('theme');q.delete('theme-at');var s=q.toString();history.replaceState(null,'',location.pathname+(s?'?'+s:'')+location.hash);}catch(e){}}})();</script>
